1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:05,790
生命到头了
The end of life.

2
00:00:05,910 --> 00:00:12,070
这个现实恐吓着我们又激励着我们
lt's a reality that terrifies us and motivates us.

3
00:00:12,180 --> 00:00:15,840
现在尖端科学正着手一个大胆的使命
Now cutting-edge science embarks on a bold mission

4
00:00:15,950 --> 00:00:18,310
来延长人的生命
to extend human life.

5
00:00:19,620 --> 00:00:22,210
有人认为答案就在生物学里
Some think the answer lies in biology.

6
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:26,610
有人认为它可能就在我们的大脑里
Some believe it might be in our brains.

7
00:00:26,730 --> 00:00:28,980
其他人则声称长生不死就意味着
And others claim that immortality would mean

8
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:31,850
人类的终结
the end of humanity.

9
00:00:31,970 --> 00:00:36,190
死亡依然是不可避免的么？
Will death remain inevitable?

10
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:38,690
亦即我们能长生不死么？
Or can we live forever?

11
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:42,660
科学频道 - 穿越虫洞 2x09
Through the Wormhole 2x09

12
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:48,410
空间 时间 生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

13
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:55,490
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie Through the Wormhole.

14
00:00:59,380 --> 00:01:03,380
我们能长生不死么？
Can We Live Forever?

15
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,090
时光之沙流逝飞速
The sands of time run swiftly,

16
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:14,230
提醒着我们人生苦短：
a reminder that life is fleeting ;

17
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:16,760
死亡乃触目惊心的现实
death is a humbling reality.

18
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:21,640
但若是生命没有尽头呢？
But what if life had no end?

19
00:01:21,750 --> 00:01:24,770
在过去的200年里
ln just the past 200 years,

20
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:26,870
平均寿命增加了一倍
the average life-span has doubled

21
00:01:26,990 --> 00:01:30,140
从40岁左右提高到了近80岁
from about 40 to almost 80 years.

22
00:01:30,260 --> 00:01:32,710
生物学和物理学的突破
Breakthroughs in biology and physics

23
00:01:32,830 --> 00:01:36,180
很快就能将长生不死纳入我们的掌控之中
could soon bring immortality within our grasp.

24
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:40,190
不管怎样 你们许多现在正看着我的人
For better or worse, many of you watching me right now

25
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,660
或许在有生之年就能看到
may live to see the day

26
00:01:41,770 --> 00:01:47,500
老死本身成为了遥远过去的遗迹
when aging and death itself are relics of a distant past.

27
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:54,000
我记得曾经翻过我祖母的箱子
l remember rummaging through my grandmother's trunk once,

28
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:58,370
发现了陈年留下的东西
happening upon objects that had been stored away for years.

29
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:04,680
所有东西都褪色 变形 锈蚀了
Everything was faded, curled, rusted.

30
00:02:04,790 --> 00:02:06,550
我禁不住纳闷起来
l couldn't help but wonder,

31
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:09,720
同样的衰变何时会发生在我的身上？
when would the same decay happen to me?

32
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:16,690
加来道雄
Michio Kaku,

33
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:19,730
纽约市立大学的理论物理学家
a theoretical physicist at City College of New York,

34
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:22,860
痴迷于科学上的大问题
is fascinated with the big questions in science,

35
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,200
诸如物理规则是否
like whether the laws of physics

36
00:02:25,310 --> 00:02:29,500
需要万物都死亡
require that all living things die.

37
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:31,340
物理学的铁律之一
One of the iron laws of physics

38
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:33,640
是热力学第二定律
is the Second Law of Thermodynamics,

39
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:35,080
其称一切都会锈蚀
which says that everything rusts,

40
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:38,510
一切都会老朽 分崩离析
everything decays, falls apart.

41
00:02:38,630 --> 00:02:41,720
我们都是原子组成的 这些原子 回过头来
We're all made out of atoms, and these atoms, in turn,

42
00:02:41,830 --> 00:02:44,160
要服从热力学第二定律
obey the Second Law of Thermodynamics.

43
00:02:46,170 --> 00:02:49,290
宇宙间任何东西 所有东西
Anything and everything in the Universe

44
00:02:49,320 --> 00:02:53,660
都趋向于从有序到无序
has the tendency to go from order to disorder.

45
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:56,260
损害一旦造成
And once the damage is done,

46
00:02:56,340 --> 00:03:01,600
事情极难逆转并且不可调和
it's extremely difficult to reverse things and un-mix them.

47
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:04,470
这个过程叫做熵
lt's a process known as entropy.

48
00:03:04,590 --> 00:03:06,840
如果我来搅拌咖啡
lf l mix coffee,

49
00:03:06,950 --> 00:03:12,850
我就意识到我将奶油放入咖啡 我就增加了熵
l realize that when l put cream into coffee, l increase entropy.

50
00:03:12,960 --> 00:03:14,320
我加大了混乱
l increase disorder.

51
00:03:14,430 --> 00:03:19,050
事实上 看到这个牛奶跳出来 重组在这个杯子里
ln fact, to see this milk jump out and reform in this cup

52
00:03:19,170 --> 00:03:21,660
是一个荒唐的事件
is such a preposterous event

53
00:03:21,770 --> 00:03:23,490
那个你所等待的时间得超过
that you would have to wait longer

54
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:27,090
宇宙的寿命才能看到它发生
than the lifetime of the Universe to see it happen.

55
00:03:27,210 --> 00:03:32,300
热力学第二定律是一个不懈之力
The Second Law of Thermodynamics is an unremitting force.

56
00:03:32,410 --> 00:03:37,070
没有什么能够规避熵的力量
Nothing is immune to the power of entropy,

57
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:39,270
甚至我们体内的细胞
not even the cells in our body.

58
00:03:39,390 --> 00:03:41,410
那就是为什么我们会变老
And that's why we age.

59
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:44,750
事实上 那就是为什么我们会死去
ln fact, that's why we die.

60
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:49,650
但事实证明熵的定律存在有漏洞
But it turns out that there is a loophole to the law of entropy.

61
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:54,520
有办法从无序恢复到有序
There is a way to restore order from disorder.

62
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:57,390
把这里每一个都都想象成代表一个原子
lmagine that each one represents an atom.

63
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:01,500
现在如果我用上热力学第二定律
Now, if l apply the Second Law of Thermodynamics,

64
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:05,370
那就意味着熵的增加 它会搅合在一起 混乱占主导
it means that entropy increases. lt mixes. Chaos reigns.

65
00:04:05,480 --> 00:04:06,930
请看
Watch this.

66
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:11,310
盘子里的糖果一开始时有序的 按颜色区分的
The candy on the tray starts off organized and color-coded.

67
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,740
但如果托盘开始晃动
But if the tray begins to vibrate,

68
00:04:13,850 --> 00:04:16,110
第二定律就接手了
the Second Law takes over.

69
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:17,980
尽管这就是熵在起作用
Even though this is entropy in action,

70
00:04:18,090 --> 00:04:22,890
我还是能够通过从外部施加能量来逆转熵
l can reverse entropy by adding energy from the outside.

71
00:04:23,830 --> 00:04:26,820
但代价是 我得始终加以留意
But the price is, l have to constantly be on alert,

72
00:04:26,930 --> 00:04:29,800
始终从外部施加能量
constantly add energy from the outside.

73
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:34,300
我说 这是个累活
l mean, this is hard work.

74
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:38,300
凭借着能量和全神贯注
With energy and concentration,

75
00:04:38,410 --> 00:04:42,800
道雄就能够干涉并阻止混乱
Michio can step in and stop the chaos.

76
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:46,140
但逆转一盘子震动的糖果的熵
But reversing entropy in a tray of vibrating candy

77
00:04:46,250 --> 00:04:49,780
远不如逆转我们体内的熵那么复杂
is far less complicated than reversing entropy in our bodies.

78
00:04:49,890 --> 00:04:52,610
我们究竟是从何开始的呢？
Where would we even begin?

79
00:04:57,130 --> 00:04:59,920
瓦尔特.朗哥在搜索答案
Valter Longo is searching for the answer.

80
00:05:00,030 --> 00:05:02,520
他是南加州大学的
He is a professor of gerontology

81
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:05,160
老年学教授
at the University of Southern California.

82
00:05:05,270 --> 00:05:10,770
他对未老先衰和英年早逝了解很多
He knows that many things live fast and die young,

83
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:14,700
他认为逆转体内熵之道的
and he believes the road to reversing entropy in the body

84
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:19,170
起点在于升温最高的地方 - 引擎里
starts where things get the hottest -- in the engine.

85
00:05:19,290 --> 00:05:22,340
在引擎里 汽油得到氧化
ln the engine, the gasoline gets o xidized,

86
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:23,840
那是个燃烧的过程
and that's a combustion process,

87
00:05:23,960 --> 00:05:26,280
这给汽车提供了能量
and this provides energy to the car.

88
00:05:26,390 --> 00:05:28,050
然而由于燃烧
But because of the combustion,

89
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:30,350
引擎本身逐渐损伤
the engine itself can become damaged,

90
00:05:30,460 --> 00:05:33,150
最终 你就不得不重建引擎
and eventually, you have to rebuild the engine.

91
00:05:33,270 --> 00:05:35,990
我们体内的每一个细胞
Every cell in our bodies

92
00:05:36,100 --> 00:05:41,600
都具有小小的引擎叫做线粒体
have tiny engines called mitochondria.

93
00:05:41,710 --> 00:05:44,630
线粒体是双膜结构
Mitochondria are double-membraned structures

94
00:05:44,750 --> 00:05:48,600
其职责就是提供能量
whose job is to provide energy.

95
00:05:48,720 --> 00:05:51,170
但一旦这些发电站发生损伤
But when these powerhouses wear down,

96
00:05:51,290 --> 00:05:55,480
我们的身体就开始衰老
our body begins to decay and age.

97
00:05:55,590 --> 00:05:59,610
所以 你可以设想在细胞里 相对应的
And so, you can imagine in the cells, instead,

98
00:05:59,730 --> 00:06:02,050
你有数百个这样的线粒体
you have hundreds of these mitochondria,

99
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:03,450
这些小引擎
these little engines.

100
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:06,550
这数百个引擎就地提供能量
And these hundreds of engines provide the energy locally

101
00:06:06,670 --> 00:06:08,820
给我们体内的每一个细胞
to every single cell in our body.

102
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:11,430
不过瓦尔特已经找到办法
But Valter has found a way

103
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:14,130
来扭转这种恶化过程
to reverse this deterioration process

104
00:06:14,240 --> 00:06:18,830
恢复小有机体内的线粒体的活力
and rejuvenate mitochondria in one tiny organism.

105
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:21,700
他已经延长面包酵母的生命 -
He has extended the life of baker's yeast --

106
00:06:21,820 --> 00:06:26,340
就是你用来做面包和啤酒的 - 到了10个星期
the kind you use to make bread and beer -- to 10 weeks.

107
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:31,880
那是酵母正常寿命6天的10倍
That's 10 times the yeast's normal life-span of 6 days.

108
00:06:31,990 --> 00:06:35,620
或许听上去并不长
lt may not sound like a long time,

109
00:06:35,730 --> 00:06:40,590
但它相当于人的寿命800岁
but it's equivalent to 800 human years.

110
00:06:40,700 --> 00:06:43,360
酵母长寿的出现
The yeast's longevity occurred

111
00:06:43,470 --> 00:06:48,700
在于两个基因 R.A.S.2 和 S.C.H.9
when two genes, R.A.S.2 and S.C.H.9,

112
00:06:48,810 --> 00:06:50,900
从它的DNA钟拿走
were removed from its DNA.

113
00:06:51,010 --> 00:06:54,970
我们在酵母中所发现的这些路径
So these pathways that we've identified in yeast,

114
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:59,410
除了促进老化外 它们还推进了DNA的损伤
in addition to promoting aging, they also promote DNA damage

115
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:00,940
并且损伤了
and the damage

116
00:07:01,050 --> 00:07:03,480
细胞内的各各种不同的系统
of a variety of different systems within the cells.

117
00:07:03,590 --> 00:07:06,310
瓦尔特不清楚此种酵母的青春之泉是否
Valter wondered if this fountain of youth for yeast

118
00:07:06,430 --> 00:07:09,850
有可能适用于更加复杂的生命形式
might apply to more complex life-forms.

119
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:13,920
于是他寻找等价基因的目光
And so he began looking for the equivalent genes

120
00:07:14,030 --> 00:07:17,420
瞄准了更大的有机体 -
in a much larger organism --

121
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:19,390
老鼠
mice.

122
00:07:19,510 --> 00:07:22,430
在他淘汰了那两个基因后
When he knocked out those two genes,

123
00:07:22,540 --> 00:07:26,300
这些老鼠的寿命增加了一倍
these mice doubled their life expectancy.

124
00:07:26,410 --> 00:07:28,470
这非常令人鼓舞
This is very encouraging

125
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:31,040
因为你不妨看一下
because if you look at the similarities

126
00:07:31,150 --> 00:07:33,740
老鼠与人类之间的类似点
between a mouse and a human,

127
00:07:33,850 --> 00:07:36,940
我们在许多方面九成以上是相同的
we are over 90% identical in many ways.

128
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:41,620
老鼠的能活2年 人能活100年
A mouse lives for 2 years, and people live for 100 years.

129
00:07:41,730 --> 00:07:42,950
所以你可以看到
So, you can see,

130
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:45,890
只要对基因组做小小的修改
with just a small modification of the genome,

131
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:48,660
你就可以从2年到100年
you can go from 2 years to 100 years.

132
00:07:48,770 --> 00:07:51,030
并没有物理定律
There's no law of physics

133
00:07:51,140 --> 00:07:54,400
阻止我们发现长寿的秘密
that prevents us from finding the secret of longevity,

134
00:07:54,510 --> 00:07:58,030
这个秘密 或许就是长生不死
the secret, perhaps, even of immortality.

135
00:07:58,150 --> 00:08:02,370
举个例子 如果你取上数以百万计的老人的基因组
lf you take, for example, the genome of millions of old people

136
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:05,040
及数以百万计的年轻人的基因组 -
and the genome of millions of young people --

137
00:08:05,150 --> 00:08:08,080
那是我们未来几年要做的事 - 相互抵减
which we will do in the coming years -- and subtract,

138
00:08:08,190 --> 00:08:12,910
你就会发现衰老聚集的基因
you will then find the genes where aging is concentrated.

139
00:08:13,030 --> 00:08:15,920
目前 我们已经确定有60个基因
Already, we've identified over 60 genes

140
00:08:16,030 --> 00:08:18,090
牵涉到衰老过程
involved in the aging process.

141
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:19,590
现在 问题是
Now, the question is,

142
00:08:19,700 --> 00:08:23,760
你如何将人的百年人生改编成
how do you reprogram a human that lives 100 years

143
00:08:23,870 --> 00:08:26,860
2000或3000岁的老人呢？
to be now a 2,000- or 3,000-year-old person?

144
00:08:28,580 --> 00:08:31,370
瓦尔特那保持我们引擎永久运行下去的使命
Valter's mission to keep our engines running forever

145
00:08:31,480 --> 00:08:35,540
刚刚开始 他将长期投身于此
has just begun, and he's in it for the long haul.

146
00:08:35,650 --> 00:08:37,240
假设我们找到了方法
Suppose we do find a way

147
00:08:37,350 --> 00:08:40,670
来保持我们的引擎运行数百年甚至更长
to keep our engines running for hundreds of years or more.

148
00:08:40,790 --> 00:08:45,150
我们都会在很长时间内老态龙钟
We would all be very old for a very long time.

149
00:08:45,260 --> 00:08:47,110
但这位科学家
But this scientist

150
00:08:47,230 --> 00:08:51,950
在寻求方法让我们不仅仅只是长生不死...
is digging for ways to keep us not just eternally alive...

151
00:08:52,070 --> 00:08:54,300
而是永葆青春
but eternally young.

152
00:08:57,030 --> 00:08:59,150
取我们目前的寿命
Take our current life-span

153
00:08:59,270 --> 00:09:03,760
将其延长5倍甚至10倍长
and stretch it 5 or even 10 times longer.

154
00:09:03,870 --> 00:09:08,530
我们青春的快乐将会持续像无尽的夏天
The joys of our youth would endure like an endless summer.

155
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:13,670
但想象一下日后的痛苦 羸弱的身躯
But imagine suffering decade after decade with fragile bones,

156
00:09:13,780 --> 00:09:18,510
衰退的视力 脆弱的心灵
failing eyesight, and an ever-more-feeble mind.

157
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:23,440
如果我们想长生不死 我们就不能光延长生命
lf we want to become immortal, we can't just extend life.

158
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:29,520
我们需要探索如何去保持我们的身体永葆青春
We need to discover how to keep our bodies eternally youthful.

159
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:38,160
奥布里.德格雷认为自己就是现代的玛士撒拉
Aubrey De Grey thinks of himself as a modern-day Methuselah.

160
00:09:38,270 --> 00:09:42,630
他毕生都致力于抗衰老
He has dedicated his career to fighting aging.

161
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:45,970
奥布里认为如今出生的许多人
Aubrey believes that many people who are born today

162
00:09:46,080 --> 00:09:48,470
能够活到1000岁
could live to be 1,000 years old

163
00:09:48,580 --> 00:09:51,970
并且大部分时间里都保持身体的青春
and remain physically young for most of that time.

164
00:09:52,090 --> 00:09:56,010
关键是良好的生物料理 -
The key is a matter of good biological housekeeping --

165
00:09:56,120 --> 00:09:59,080
清除我们体内的垃圾
taking the trash out of ourselves.

166
00:09:59,190 --> 00:10:00,750
结果的确就是
So, what happens, indeed,

167
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:03,750
体内某种材料
is that certain types of material in the body

168
00:10:03,860 --> 00:10:05,890
就是堆积的垃圾
just accumulate junk.

169
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:10,290
某种垃圾堆积在细胞内部及细胞间
Certain types of junk accumulate inside cells and between cells

170
00:10:10,400 --> 00:10:12,930
我们并不认为是垃圾的东西 如DNA
and stuff that we would not think of as junk, like DNA,

171
00:10:13,040 --> 00:10:17,330
任意堆积 那就是一类垃圾
accumulates randomness, which is a type of junk.

172
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:19,400
细胞有项重要职责
Cells have the important job

173
00:10:19,510 --> 00:10:21,770
就是不断分解废物
of constantly breaking down waste,

174
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:24,200
它们几乎都擅长于此
and they're mostly pretty good at it.

175
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:25,340
但有时细胞
But sometimes a cell

176
00:10:25,450 --> 00:10:28,040
遇到的东西会非常怪异...
comes across things that are so weird...

177
00:10:28,160 --> 00:10:31,480
这种降解机械对它们毫无作用
none of this degradation machinery works on them.

178
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:33,510
此时垃圾会重新通向
That's when the junk gets re-routed

179
00:10:33,630 --> 00:10:35,820
所谓的溶酶体 -
to what's called the lysosome,

180
00:10:35,930 --> 00:10:37,720
细胞里容纳
a vessel that houses

181
00:10:37,830 --> 00:10:41,190
最强大降解机械的容器
the most powerful degradation machinery in the cell.

182
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:43,790
如果垃圾得不到分解
And if the garbage can't be broken down,

183
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:46,530
那就会永远堆在那里
it stays there forever.

184
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:52,170
奥布里认为垃圾堆积在
Aubrey believes that the accumulation of garbage

185
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:55,900
溶酶体的存储单元里就是衰老的肇因
in the lysosomal storage unit is what causes aging.

186
00:10:56,020 --> 00:10:59,940
他想将我们体内的这些堆积物释放出来
And he wants to free our bodies of these buildups.

187
00:11:00,050 --> 00:11:01,910
他认为最合乎逻辑的方法
He realized the most logical way

188
00:11:02,020 --> 00:11:05,450
就是仔细看看这些废物
was to take a close look at waste itself.

189
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:11,490
他想知道 在自然界 有什么喜欢吃垃圾？
And he wondered, "ln nature, what likes to eat junk?"

190
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:13,620
我们在研究一个想法
We're working on an idea

191
00:11:13,730 --> 00:11:16,190
我们或许能够找到别的物种
that we might be able to find other species,

192
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:20,030
尤其是细菌 能够分解
especially bacteria, that are able to break down

193
00:11:20,140 --> 00:11:23,500
堆积在人体内的物质
the substances that accumulate in the human body.

194
00:11:23,610 --> 00:11:26,830
奥布里追寻青春之泉
Aubrey's hunt for the fountain of youth

195
00:11:26,950 --> 00:11:31,440
将其引至最后的安息之地 - 墓地
led him to a final resting place -- graveyards.

196
00:11:33,290 --> 00:11:34,840
由于墓地到处都是
Since graveyards are riddled

197
00:11:34,960 --> 00:11:37,850
白骨累累
with the waste of decomposing bodies,

198
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:40,250
他怀疑他有可能找到
he suspected he might find microbes

199
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:42,950
寄生在尸体上的微生物
who live to feast on death.

200
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:48,420
如果我们能够找到它们用来
And if we can find the genes and enzymes that they are using

201
00:11:48,540 --> 00:11:50,420
实际发挥作用的基因和酶
to actually perform that function,

202
00:11:50,540 --> 00:11:53,560
那么我们就有可能将那些基因和酶
then we might be able to put those genes and enzymes

203
00:11:53,670 --> 00:11:55,730
放入我们自己的细胞里
into our own cells

204
00:11:55,840 --> 00:11:57,730
这样我们自己的细胞就能分解
so that our own cells can break stuff down

205
00:11:57,840 --> 00:11:59,670
那些不能自己自然分解的东西
that they could not naturally break down.

206
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:03,170
长寿的秘密有可能就在6呎之下
The secret to longevity might be 6 feet under.

207
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:08,910
奥布里及其团队仍然在搜寻合适的微生物
Aubrey and his team are still digging for the right microbe

208
00:12:09,020 --> 00:12:10,410
将会引进到哺乳动物的细胞里
that will work in mammalian cells.

209
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:12,280
他们一旦找到
And when they find it,

210
00:12:12,390 --> 00:12:18,190
他认为我们都将永远生活在25岁时
he believes we will all live like 25-year-olds forever.

211
00:12:18,300 --> 00:12:21,990
我认为我们在接下来的25年左右
l think that we have maybe a 50/50 chance

212
00:12:22,100 --> 00:12:24,690
或许有50对50的机会
in the next 25 years or so

213
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:27,660
来开发我所认为的第一代
of developing what l think of as the first generation

214
00:12:27,770 --> 00:12:30,430
真正的返老还童的生物技术
of bona fide rejuvenation biotechnology.

215
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:31,730
换句话说
ln other words,

216
00:12:31,850 --> 00:12:34,370
有了足够全面的技术
technologies that are sufficiently comprehensive

217
00:12:34,480 --> 00:12:37,940
我们将它们带给中年人
that we can give them to middle-aged people,

218
00:12:38,050 --> 00:12:41,780
六七十岁的人 将他们照料得健健康康
people 60 or 70 or so, and fix them up well enough

219
00:12:41,890 --> 00:12:45,580
他们就不会再次变成生物学上六七十岁
that they don't become biologically 60 or 70 again

220
00:12:45,690 --> 00:12:48,130
直到30年之后还是那样
until 30 years later or something like that.

221
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:54,450
奥布里在寻求抗衰老的妙法
Aubrey is looking for the magic bullet that will fight aging,

222
00:12:54,570 --> 00:12:57,060
但合成生物学家克里斯.沃伊特
but synthetic biologist Chris Voigt

223
00:12:57,170 --> 00:13:00,330
正在寻求构建整个部队
is looking to build an entire army

224
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:03,500
用来自整个自然界里的各个部件
out of various parts from all over nature.

225
00:13:05,710 --> 00:13:09,270
好 我们来试试
All right. Let's give this a shot.

226
00:13:13,690 --> 00:13:15,910
我们在合成生物学里经常做的一件事
One of the things we're often trying to do

227
00:13:16,020 --> 00:13:18,310
创造新的功能
in synthetic biology is create new functions

228
00:13:18,430 --> 00:13:21,920
采用已经存在于自然界里的用于其他目的的部件
out of parts that already exist in nature for other reasons.

229
00:13:22,030 --> 00:13:25,350
所以我们经常要走出去捕获这些部件
So we're often having to go out and grab those components

230
00:13:25,470 --> 00:13:28,260
它们来自不同的生物体 并将它们放在一块
from different organisms and put them together.

231
00:13:28,370 --> 00:13:31,230
克里斯搜寻微生物
Chris searches for microorganisms

232
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:35,060
通过生物学来找到合适的部件
throughout biology to find the right parts.

233
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:37,530
它们归宿到他的实验室
And they end up at his lab

234
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:40,670
位于旧金山的加州大学
at the University of California, San Francisco,

235
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:43,970
在那里生物学遇上了机械学
where biology meets mechanics.

236
00:13:44,080 --> 00:13:46,810
你看人类能够抗争疾病
When you look at being able to fight disease,

237
00:13:46,920 --> 00:13:48,680
不管是否能识别恶性细胞与否
whether it's identifying malignant cells

238
00:13:48,790 --> 00:13:50,110
并且杀死它们
and killing them,

239
00:13:50,220 --> 00:13:52,380
我们努力搜查垃圾的全部作用
we're trying to go through the garbage of all the functions

240
00:13:52,490 --> 00:13:53,880
它们都出自自然界
that are out in the natural world

241
00:13:53,990 --> 00:13:57,420
找出那些对我们有用的
and identify those that are useful to us

242
00:13:57,530 --> 00:13:59,550
努力做到能够认出
in trying to be able to identify

243
00:13:59,670 --> 00:14:02,120
体内正确的病态
a correct disease state in the body.

244
00:14:02,240 --> 00:14:05,960
为了构造能够检测器周围环境的生物机器人
To build a bio robot that can detect its surroundings,

245
00:14:06,070 --> 00:14:09,930
克里斯及其团队需要找到带有传感器的有机体
Chris and his team needed to find an organism with a sensor.

246
00:14:10,040 --> 00:14:14,170
他们找到了 就在一潭死水里...
And they found it... in pond scum...

247
00:14:14,280 --> 00:14:19,140
他们发现其中的藻类都配备有光传感器
where they discovered that algae are equipped with light sensors.

248
00:14:19,250 --> 00:14:22,380
我们用一种细菌 它通常生长在你的肠道里 -
We took a bacterium that normally lives in your gut --

249
00:14:22,490 --> 00:14:24,980
所以它无需光照 -
so it's not used to the Sun shining --

250
00:14:25,090 --> 00:14:28,020
我们放入一个来自藻类的光传感器
and we put in a light sensor out of an algae.

251
00:14:28,130 --> 00:14:31,820
在克里斯的细菌暗房里
lnside Chris' bacterial darkroom,

252
00:14:31,930 --> 00:14:33,990
菌落
colonies of bacteria

253
00:14:34,100 --> 00:14:35,790
现在植入了藻类光传感器
that are now implanted with algae light sensors

254
00:14:35,900 --> 00:14:39,240
能够拍摄到投影图像
are able to take photographs of slide-projected images.

255
00:14:41,510 --> 00:14:44,100
看上去像是细菌艺术
lt might look like bacterial art,

256
00:14:44,210 --> 00:14:46,230
但这是迈向
but this is the first big step

257
00:14:46,350 --> 00:14:49,540
打造程控生物机器人的第一大步
toward creating programmable biological robots

258
00:14:49,650 --> 00:14:52,210
它将让我们的身体永葆健康
that will keep our bodies healthy forever.

259
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:55,740
细菌遍布我们体内
There are bacteria all throughout our bodies,

260
00:14:55,860 --> 00:14:58,850
我们能够改编这些细菌
and we can reprogram those bacteria

261
00:14:58,960 --> 00:15:02,050
目的是能够实施治疗效果
in order to be able to implement therapeutic effects

262
00:15:02,160 --> 00:15:05,520
以对付你能够想到的任何疾病
for just about any disease that you can imagine.

263
00:15:05,630 --> 00:15:09,460
可能性是无极限的
The possibilities are limitless.

264
00:15:09,570 --> 00:15:12,160
克里斯的摄影细菌
Chris' photographic bacteria

265
00:15:12,270 --> 00:15:14,390
能够自我开关
are able to turn themselves on and off

266
00:15:14,510 --> 00:15:15,860
就像开关那样
like the flip of a switch.

267
00:15:15,980 --> 00:15:17,430
但问题是
But the question is,

268
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:22,870
克里斯能够解决如何去程控那个开关么？
can Chris figure out how to program and control that switch?

269
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:24,970
我们希望看到细胞的编程
We'd like to see the programming of cells

270
00:15:25,080 --> 00:15:26,910
如计算机编程
be the same as programming a computer

271
00:15:27,020 --> 00:15:28,440
设计电子芯片那样
or designing an electronic chip,

272
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:31,110
这样 作为程序员 我们就会写出
where, as programmers, we would write out

273
00:15:31,220 --> 00:15:34,280
我们所希望细胞要实现的确切功能
the exact function that we'd want the cells to do.

274
00:15:34,390 --> 00:15:37,180
然后就会自动编译进
And then that would be automatically compiled

275
00:15:37,300 --> 00:15:38,490
DNA序列里
into a DNA sequence

276
00:15:38,600 --> 00:15:40,590
以计算机程序同样的方式
in the same way that a computer program

277
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:44,020
进行编译 最终 就是1和0
gets compiled, ultimately, into ones and zeroes.

278
00:15:44,140 --> 00:15:45,360
那就是梦想
That's the dream.

279
00:15:48,010 --> 00:15:51,000
生物机器人有可能有朝一日漫游在我们血管里
Bio robots may one day roam our blood streams,

280
00:15:51,110 --> 00:15:55,540
监视我们的器官 响应我们体内的报警
police our organs, and respond to our internal 911 calls,

281
00:15:55,650 --> 00:15:59,610
让我们免遭病魔的威胁
keeping us free from the threat of disease.

282
00:16:01,350 --> 00:16:04,510
克里斯.沃伊特及奥布里.德格雷
Chris Voigt's and Aubrey De Grey's research

283
00:16:04,620 --> 00:16:06,750
让我们永葆健康的研究
to keep our bodies healthy for eternity

284
00:16:06,860 --> 00:16:08,820
还处于起步阶段
is still in the beginning stages.

285
00:16:08,930 --> 00:16:11,950
然而这是否意味着对我们凡人而言
But what does this mean for us mere mortals

286
00:16:12,070 --> 00:16:14,520
将永远活不到
who will never live to see the day

287
00:16:14,630 --> 00:16:16,960
长生不死指日可待之时了呢？
when immortality is within reach?

288
00:16:17,070 --> 00:16:20,090
有一个令人毛骨悚然的可能性
There is one chilling possibility

289
00:16:20,210 --> 00:16:26,670
将会给我们大家一个搁置起来的机会 即使我们已不在世
that will give us all a chance to hang on, even after we die.

290
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:32,050
我们全都是基因里编就了
We're all genetically programmed

291
00:16:32,170 --> 00:16:36,770
贪生怕死
to lust for life and to flee from death.

292
00:16:37,040 --> 00:16:40,780
最终 我们将会发现长生的秘密
Eventually, we will discover the secret of immortality,

293
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:43,090
但是我们现在还没有
but we're not there yet.

294
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:48,190
为了现在就诈死 我们需要将年龄增长冰封起来
To cheat death right now, we need to put aging on ice

295
00:16:48,300 --> 00:16:52,630
准备着在生命放弃我们很久之后
and be ready to grasp eternal life

296
00:16:52,740 --> 00:16:56,010
把握永生
long after life abandons us.

297
00:16:59,250 --> 00:17:01,900
格雷格.法伊是一位低温生物学家
Greg Fahy is a cryobiologist.

298
00:17:02,020 --> 00:17:07,480
他的小组在21世纪医学生物技术装备的目标
The goal of his team at Biotech Outfit 21 st Century Medicine

299
00:17:07,590 --> 00:17:10,650
是冻结人体器官组织
is to freeze human organs and tissues

300
00:17:10,760 --> 00:17:15,420
以使它们在今后若干世纪后能够复苏 毫发无损
so that they can be revived, undamaged, centuries from now.

301
00:17:17,070 --> 00:17:19,520
超低温储存是在极低温下的
Cryopreservation is the preservation of living systems

302
00:17:19,640 --> 00:17:21,360
活体储存系统
at very low temperatures.

303
00:17:21,470 --> 00:17:24,830
我们通常提及的温度是低到足以
We usually are referring to temperatures that are low enough

304
00:17:24,940 --> 00:17:28,100
让你能够储存的系统长到随你怎么想
that you can store the system as long as you wish

305
00:17:28,210 --> 00:17:29,730
然后让你再次去用它
before you use it again.

306
00:17:29,850 --> 00:17:32,000
超低温储存关系到
Cryopreservation is essential

307
00:17:32,110 --> 00:17:34,440
把一个细胞 或组织 器官
to get a cell or a tissue, an organ,

308
00:17:34,550 --> 00:17:37,440
从某时某地到另一时另一地
from one place in time to another place in time

309
00:17:37,550 --> 00:17:41,340
不让该系统在这个过程中发生变化
without allowing that system to change in the process.

310
00:17:41,460 --> 00:17:43,610
科学家几十年来一直在尝试
Scientists have been trying

311
00:17:43,730 --> 00:17:46,320
保存整个器官
to preserve whole organs for decades.

312
00:17:46,430 --> 00:17:50,150
但是其难度远甚于将将食品扔进冰箱
But it's a lot harder than throwing food in the freezer.

313
00:17:50,270 --> 00:17:56,030
生物物质冰冻时 冰晶形成
When biological material freezes, ice crystals form,

314
00:17:56,140 --> 00:17:59,630
将会把细胞推出其正常的位置
which push the cells out of their normal position.

315
00:17:59,740 --> 00:18:03,970
你解冻器官后 可能外表看上去很好
When you thaw the organ, it may look okay from the outside,

316
00:18:04,080 --> 00:18:08,410
但里头 已经损伤无法修复了
but on the inside, it's damaged beyond repair.

317
00:18:10,550 --> 00:18:13,610
最大的问题 我们认为 其实在于力学
The biggest problem, we think, is really mechanical.

318
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:16,180
就是细胞间冰的构成
lt's the formation of ice between cells.

319
00:18:16,290 --> 00:18:19,350
如果细胞被赶出了其正常位置
lf the cells are dislodged from their normal locations,

320
00:18:19,460 --> 00:18:20,580
你就可能破坏了
then you can destroy

321
00:18:20,700 --> 00:18:23,250
整体结构的功能
the function of that structure as a whole

322
00:18:23,370 --> 00:18:24,960
哪怕细胞还活着
even if the cells survive.

323
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:30,130
格雷格及其团队决定将他们的努力集中在
Greg and his team decided to focus their efforts

324
00:18:30,240 --> 00:18:32,030
防止体液的冻结
on preventing bodily fluids from freezing

325
00:18:32,140 --> 00:18:34,130
最终开发出方法
and eventually developed a way

326
00:18:34,240 --> 00:18:39,000
将那些液体转化成一种生物玻璃 -
to turn those fluids into a form of biological glass --

327
00:18:39,110 --> 00:18:42,970
该技术他称之为玻璃化
a technique he calls vitrification.

328
00:18:43,090 --> 00:18:46,210
玻璃化是玻璃的构造
Vitrification is the formation of a glass.

329
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:49,610
所以你如果取水 将其混合进各种化学品中
So if you take water and you mix it with various chemicals

330
00:18:49,730 --> 00:18:51,850
使其具有足够的化学浓度
in high-enough concentrations of chemical

331
00:18:51,960 --> 00:18:54,380
然后将其降至低温
and then cool it down to low temperatures,

332
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:57,520
系统将永不冻结 不管你温度有多低
the system will never freeze no matter how low you go.

333
00:18:57,630 --> 00:18:58,990
为了测试
To test whether

334
00:18:59,100 --> 00:19:03,290
玻璃化是否确实保护了整个器官的功能
vitrification actually preserves the function of whole organs,

335
00:19:03,410 --> 00:19:07,430
格雷格小组玻璃化了一只兔子的肾脏
Greg's team vitrified a rabbit kidney.

336
00:19:07,540 --> 00:19:10,770
这里时肾丛生实验室
This is the kidney profusion lab.

337
00:19:10,880 --> 00:19:12,940
约翰在后台所做的
What John is doing in the background

338
00:19:13,050 --> 00:19:16,070
是将其...透过肾脏的血管系统
is delivering...through the vascular system of the kidney

339
00:19:16,190 --> 00:19:18,440
这样肾脏就不会冻结了
so the kidney becomes unfreezable.

340
00:19:18,550 --> 00:19:21,610
肾脏放在一个特殊的容器里
The kidney is sitting in a special chamber.

341
00:19:21,720 --> 00:19:23,080
该容器调温至
The chamber is temperature-conditioned

342
00:19:23,190 --> 00:19:24,450
最低的毒性
to minimize to xicity.

343
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:26,280
此过程结束后
And at the end of the process,

344
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:28,380
肾脏就被取出进行移植
the kidney's taken out and is transplanted.

345
00:19:28,500 --> 00:19:30,990
这个肾脏
This kidney,

346
00:19:31,100 --> 00:19:36,090
尽管一直被储存在-22摄氏度
even though it has been stored at minus-22 degrees Celsius,

347
00:19:36,210 --> 00:19:38,900
其功能一如正常的肾脏
works just as well as a normal kidney.

348
00:19:39,010 --> 00:19:40,060
这证明了
lt's proof

349
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:43,530
格雷格的玻璃化冷冻技术的实际功效
that Greg's vitrification technique actually works

350
00:19:43,650 --> 00:19:44,930
有可能成为
and might be the key

351
00:19:45,050 --> 00:19:50,070
将人类的器官保存至未来的关键
to preserving human organs far into the future.

352
00:19:50,190 --> 00:19:53,940
如果你能将温度降至玻璃化的温度
So if you cool to below that glass-transition temperature,

353
00:19:54,060 --> 00:19:56,710
我们的计算表明你就可以储存一个系统
our calculations indicate you can store a system

354
00:19:56,830 --> 00:19:58,480
达数万年之久
for tens of thousands of years.

355
00:19:58,590 --> 00:20:03,020
想想能够将你的躯体放在冰内
lmagine being able to put your body on ice

356
00:20:03,130 --> 00:20:07,820
然后在万年之后进行复苏
and then being revived 10,000 years from now.

357
00:20:07,940 --> 00:20:10,330
你会醒来发现自己
You could wake up and find yourself

358
00:20:10,440 --> 00:20:15,160
已经身处在了科学使长生成为可能的时代
in an age when science has made immortality possible.

359
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:17,530
格雷格.法伊可能才刚刚起步
Greg Fahy may be just a few years

360
00:20:17,650 --> 00:20:22,140
尚无法给一些特定人员打上一针永生的强心剂
from giving a few select people a shot at eternal life.

361
00:20:22,250 --> 00:20:23,970
但是那只能发生在
But that can only happen

362
00:20:24,090 --> 00:20:27,240
超低温冷冻能否整个人体对有效
if cryopreservation works on an entire human body,

363
00:20:27,360 --> 00:20:30,610
不仅仅是单个器官 如肾脏或膀胱
not just simple organs like the kidney or the bladder,

364
00:20:30,730 --> 00:20:36,290
而是对其中最复杂的器官 - 大脑
but on the most complex organ of all -- the brain.

365
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:38,820
这是脑切片玻璃化实验室
This is the brain-slice vitrification lab.

366
00:20:38,930 --> 00:20:41,820
实验对象是一片海马脑切片 -
The setup shows a hippocampal slice --

367
00:20:41,940 --> 00:20:45,260
那部分大脑与学习记忆有关 -
the part of the brain that's associated with learning and memory --

368
00:20:45,370 --> 00:20:48,160
放在小碟子里 基本上是这样
in a little dish, essentially.

369
00:20:48,280 --> 00:20:51,170
在右边 我们有一个刺激电极
On the right side, we have a stimulating electrode coming in,

370
00:20:51,280 --> 00:20:52,470
刺入切片
piercing the slice.

371
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:55,550
左边 我们有一个记录电极
On the left side, we have a recording electrode.

372
00:20:56,990 --> 00:21:00,640
这片切片已经被玻璃化了
This brain slice has been vitrified.

373
00:21:00,760 --> 00:21:02,610
如果它仍然具有功能
lf it's still functional,

374
00:21:02,730 --> 00:21:06,080
电脉冲射入其中后
when a pulse of electricity is fired into it,

375
00:21:06,200 --> 00:21:08,280
它应该会反射一个信号
it should fire a signal back.

376
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:12,050
让格雷格及其团队惊喜不已的是 确实这样
To Greg and his team's surprise, it does.

377
00:21:12,170 --> 00:21:16,830
电活动确实点燃了这片脑组织
Electrical activity actually sparks up in this brain tissue.

378
00:21:16,940 --> 00:21:18,560
电反应
And the electrical response

379
00:21:18,670 --> 00:21:23,900
完全强似其原来的状态 -
is every bit as strong as it was in its original state --

380
00:21:24,010 --> 00:21:27,740
脑切片完全复苏了
the recovery of the brain slice complete.

381
00:21:27,850 --> 00:21:30,970
保存一片大脑
Preserving a slice of the brain

382
00:21:31,090 --> 00:21:35,750
是接下来保存整个东西关键的第一步
is a crucial first step toward preserving the whole thing.

383
00:21:35,860 --> 00:21:40,280
这个雄心勃勃的项目 格雷格已经在着手了
lt's an ambitious project that Greg is already working on.

384
00:21:40,400 --> 00:21:44,920
我们发现玻璃化大脑相当容易
We have found that it's fairly easy to vitrify the brain,

385
00:21:45,030 --> 00:21:48,720
整个结构似乎都得到了保护
that all of the structure seems to be preserved.

386
00:21:48,840 --> 00:21:50,830
但是我得说迄今为止
But l have to say that so far,

387
00:21:50,940 --> 00:21:53,500
我们的技术水平尚达不到
our techniques are not up to the level

388
00:21:53,610 --> 00:21:56,970
我们所希望的那种大胆的想法
that we would like for that kind of speculative idea.

389
00:21:57,080 --> 00:21:59,600
但是我不能说 你无法弥补
But l couldn't say that you couldn't make up

390
00:21:59,720 --> 00:22:01,840
我们今天保鲜技术中的
for whatever deficiencies

391
00:22:01,950 --> 00:22:04,710
任何不足
that we have in our preservation technique today

392
00:22:04,820 --> 00:22:07,380
如果利用某些未知的未来科技的话
using some unknown future technology.

393
00:22:07,490 --> 00:22:10,050
我不能排除那是一种可能性
l can't rule that out as a possibility.

394
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:19,620
有朝一日 我们与我们的亲人告别了几百年后
One day, centuries after we say goodbye to our loved ones,

395
00:22:19,740 --> 00:22:22,990
我们冻结的身躯能够重新复活
our frozen bodies could be reanimated,

396
00:22:23,110 --> 00:22:26,030
我们将重返人间
and we'll walk the Earth again.

397
00:22:26,140 --> 00:22:29,170
不过可能有更好的路线达到长生
But there could be a better route to immortality,

398
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:30,830
它会改变
one that would change

399
00:22:30,950 --> 00:22:34,580
意义上的人类的根本属性
the very nature of what it means to be human.

400
00:22:37,070 --> 00:22:39,800
试想一下如果从来没人死去过
lmagine if no one ever died.

401
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:42,720
我们都将活在何处？
Where would we all live?

402
00:22:42,830 --> 00:22:46,110
我们的星球早已人们为患了
Our planet is already crowded enough.

403
00:22:46,220 --> 00:22:48,210
然而或许有一种长生不死之道
But maybe there is a way to live forever

404
00:22:48,320 --> 00:22:51,980
根本无需这个累赘的皮囊
without all this excess baggage.

405
00:22:52,090 --> 00:22:55,590
诗人说我们的灵魂在这里
Poets say the essence of us is here,

406
00:22:55,700 --> 00:23:00,890
但我知道真正让我成为我的全都在这里
but l know what really makes me me is all up here.

407
00:23:01,220 --> 00:23:04,480
因此如果我想坚持永生
So if l want to endure for eternity,

408
00:23:04,590 --> 00:23:07,990
或许那就是我需要紧抓不放的
perhaps that's all l need to hang on to.

409
00:23:08,100 --> 00:23:12,320
如果我们能找到方法上传我们的大脑
What if we could find a way to upload our brains,

410
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:15,930
数字化我们本人的灵魂又会怎样呢？
to digitize the very essence of ourselves?

411
00:23:16,040 --> 00:23:20,560
我们的头脑能够继续活着 虽然我们的肉体已经死去很久
Our minds could go on living long after our flesh has died.

412
00:23:20,680 --> 00:23:22,100
但要做到这一点
But to make that happen,

413
00:23:22,210 --> 00:23:25,040
我们需要了解大脑的结构
we need to understand the brain's architecture

414
00:23:25,150 --> 00:23:29,180
搞明白是什么真正让我们成为了我们
and figure out what truly makes us who we are.

415
00:23:31,420 --> 00:23:36,480
奥拉夫.斯鲍恩斯是印第安纳大学的神经学家
Olaf Sporns is a neuroscientist at lndiana University.

416
00:23:36,590 --> 00:23:41,550
他正试图解读大脑错综复杂的网络
He is attempting to unscramble the brain's tangled web.

417
00:23:41,670 --> 00:23:43,190
大脑宛如一个大城市
The brain is like a big city.

418
00:23:43,300 --> 00:23:46,120
城市是典型的复杂系统 -
Cities are examples of complex systems --

419
00:23:46,240 --> 00:23:48,460
数以千计的居民 他们的社会交往
thousands of inhabitants and their social interactions,

420
00:23:48,570 --> 00:23:49,630
他们的物流
their flow of materials.

421
00:23:49,740 --> 00:23:52,930
大脑就是那样的 - 数以百万计的神经元
The brain is like that -- millions of neurons,

422
00:23:53,040 --> 00:23:55,400
这些神经元之间的互动
interactions between these neurons.

423
00:23:55,510 --> 00:23:58,700
一定意义上 你的大脑就像是思想的城市
ln a sense, your brain is like a city of the mind.

424
00:23:58,820 --> 00:24:00,770
人脑
The human brain

425
00:24:00,880 --> 00:24:04,310
是宇宙中最复杂的系统之一
is one of the most complex systems in the Universe.

426
00:24:04,420 --> 00:24:08,550
如果你将其电气线路延展出去
lf you stretched out all of its electrical wiring,

427
00:24:08,660 --> 00:24:11,980
会从地球延伸到月球
it would extend from the Earth to the Moon.

428
00:24:12,100 --> 00:24:15,490
搞明白我们细胞及线路是如何联系上
Figuring out how we get from cells and wires

429
00:24:15,600 --> 00:24:17,990
思想和记忆的
to thoughts and memories

430
00:24:18,100 --> 00:24:22,490
是科学上最大的挑战之一
is one of the greatest challenges known to science.

431
00:24:22,610 --> 00:24:24,800
尽管我们一直在研究人脑
Even though we've been studying the human brain,

432
00:24:24,910 --> 00:24:26,700
真的 几十年来 或许几百年来
really, for decades, perhaps centuries,

433
00:24:26,810 --> 00:24:29,370
我们还是没有得到它如何联系在一起的复杂地图
we still don't have a complete map of how it's connected.

434
00:24:29,480 --> 00:24:32,400
奥拉夫正在接受此项挑战
But Olaf is taking on this challenge.

435
00:24:32,520 --> 00:24:37,210
他的目标是绘制每一个神经元和突触
His goal is to chart every single neuron and synapse

436
00:24:37,320 --> 00:24:41,450
创建一幅完整的大脑地图 叫做脑连接组
and create a complete map of the brain, called the connectome.

437
00:24:41,560 --> 00:24:43,710
那是整套连接
lt's a comprehensive set of connections

438
00:24:43,830 --> 00:24:45,650
将使我们首次
that will allow us for the first time

439
00:24:45,760 --> 00:24:47,050
更加详尽地了解
to understand in more detail

440
00:24:47,160 --> 00:24:49,390
大脑区域相互之间是如何联系的
how brain regions are connected to each other.

441
00:24:49,500 --> 00:24:51,920
奥拉夫创建脑连接组
Olaf is creating the connectome

442
00:24:52,040 --> 00:24:56,030
所使用的领先技术叫做弥散成像
using a leading-edge technique called diffusion imaging.

443
00:24:56,140 --> 00:24:58,860
它揭示大脑的长距离联系
lt reveals the brain's long-distance connections

444
00:24:58,980 --> 00:25:02,070
是通过跟踪沿着神经高速公路走的水分子
by tracking water molecules along the neural highways.

445
00:25:02,180 --> 00:25:05,770
所呈现的是核心地带
And what emerges is a detailed map

446
00:25:05,880 --> 00:25:09,210
脑细胞联系的详细地图
of the central core of brain-cell connections.

447
00:25:09,320 --> 00:25:14,310
奥拉夫认为这里就是我们个性所在的区域
Olaf thinks this is the area where our personality resides.

448
00:25:14,420 --> 00:25:16,510
我们最近已经发现
As we've been discovering recently,

449
00:25:16,630 --> 00:25:19,020
有些大脑区域的联系要多于其他区域
some brain regions are more connected than others.

450
00:25:19,130 --> 00:25:21,020
有些更加关键 或许
Some are more essential, perhaps,

451
00:25:21,130 --> 00:25:22,990
关系到大脑的整体运作
for the functioning of the brain as a whole.

452
00:25:23,100 --> 00:25:26,290
那些区域 我们称为枢纽
Those regions, we call hubs.

453
00:25:26,400 --> 00:25:27,980
如果说大脑是座城市...
lf the brain is like a city...

454
00:25:29,210 --> 00:25:31,360
...枢纽就是主要的路口
...hubs are like major intersections

455
00:25:31,470 --> 00:25:34,060
这里交通流量频繁
where a constant flow of traffic passes through,

456
00:25:34,180 --> 00:25:36,530
把人们从一地送往另一地
getting people from one place to another.

457
00:25:38,050 --> 00:25:40,700
这些路口至关重要 -
These intersections are so essential --

458
00:25:40,820 --> 00:25:45,140
如果它们遭受任何破坏 整座城市就将瘫痪
if they become disrupted in any way, the whole city shuts down.

459
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:50,980
就其性质而言 枢纽就是信息传送的聚焦点
By their nature, hubs are focal points of information traffic.

460
00:25:51,090 --> 00:25:53,420
信息汇集到这些区域
lnformation converges on these regions,

461
00:25:53,530 --> 00:25:56,020
此起彼伏的信息
and it is that ebb and flow of information

462
00:25:56,130 --> 00:25:58,560
巨大的信息流
and the magnitude of the information flow

463
00:25:58,670 --> 00:26:00,460
从大脑的其他区域
that really sets these hubs apart

464
00:26:00,570 --> 00:26:02,290
全都汇集到这些枢纽
from other regions of the brain.

465
00:26:02,410 --> 00:26:04,360
奥拉夫正越来越接近
Olaf is getting closer

466
00:26:04,470 --> 00:26:07,360
摸清意识的所在地了
to figuring out where consciousness resides.

467
00:26:07,480 --> 00:26:10,270
寻找意识的起源
Searching for the origins of consciousness

468
00:26:10,380 --> 00:26:12,970
是神经科学的圣杯
is the holy grail of neuroscience.

469
00:26:13,080 --> 00:26:16,810
一旦这个谜团得到解决 我们就能够复制人的大脑
Only when this mystery is solved can we replicate the human brain

470
00:26:16,920 --> 00:26:19,610
取出个人的意识
and take consciousness from a person

471
00:26:19,720 --> 00:26:24,880
将其输入机器作为永生的手段
and transfer it to a machine as a way to live for eternity.

472
00:26:24,990 --> 00:26:29,090
奥拉夫认为他已经发现了
And Olaf believes he has discovered a hub

473
00:26:29,200 --> 00:26:34,160
有可能解开这个谜团的一个枢纽 - 内侧顶叶皮层
that might solve this mystery -- the medial parietal cortex.

474
00:26:34,270 --> 00:26:37,860
它位于大脑的两个半球之间
lt's located between the brain's two hemispheres.

475
00:26:37,970 --> 00:26:43,370
奥拉夫怀疑它有可能藏有我们的灵魂
Olaf suspects it might house the essence of who we are.

476
00:26:43,480 --> 00:26:46,540
大脑大量的导线汇集到该处
A lot of wires from the brain converge on that region.

477
00:26:46,650 --> 00:26:48,210
有许多证据链
There are many lines of evidence

478
00:26:48,320 --> 00:26:50,370
指向该部分区域
that point to that part of the brain

479
00:26:50,490 --> 00:26:52,580
是真正的核心 极其重要 -
being really central and really important --

480
00:26:52,690 --> 00:26:55,210
甚至关系到认知和意识
perhaps even for awareness and consciousness.

481
00:26:56,890 --> 00:26:58,050
奥拉夫认为
Olaf believes

482
00:26:58,160 --> 00:27:01,990
他可能已经解开了意识之谜
he may have solved the secrets of consciousness,

483
00:27:02,100 --> 00:27:06,120
但还有更多的工作做完 才能够使他得到肯定
but there is much more work to be done before he can be sure.

484
00:27:06,240 --> 00:27:08,830
我认为我们越来越接近解答
l think we're getting closer to answering this

485
00:27:08,940 --> 00:27:09,490
这个问题了
question.

486
00:27:09,610 --> 00:27:12,900
随着我们确定了哪部分大脑关系到
And as we identify which parts of the brain are critical

487
00:27:13,010 --> 00:27:15,900
实现特定的功能
in bringing about that particular functionality,

488
00:27:16,010 --> 00:27:18,270
那就让我们更加接近回答这个问题：
that gets us closer to answering the question,

489
00:27:18,380 --> 00:27:19,600
意识是什么？
"What is consciousness?"

490
00:27:19,720 --> 00:27:22,310
以及 究竟是大脑的哪些关键部位
And also "Where are the critical components of the brain

491
00:27:22,420 --> 00:27:23,870
促成了它？
that contribute to it?"

492
00:27:25,190 --> 00:27:27,710
神经科学已经朝着绘制大脑
Neuroscience has taken enormous steps

493
00:27:27,820 --> 00:27:29,150
以及解决意识之谜
towards mapping the brain

494
00:27:29,260 --> 00:27:31,520
迈出了巨大的步子
and solving the riddle of consciousness.

495
00:27:31,630 --> 00:27:34,180
但是我们究竟能否捕获到
But will we ever be able to capture

496
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:37,590
数万亿个神经元 数十亿个突触
the trillions of synapses, billions of neurons,

497
00:27:37,700 --> 00:27:41,830
把我们自己下载到机器里呢？
and download ourselves onto a machine?

498
00:27:41,940 --> 00:27:44,730
只要想象一下发生在现实世界里的所有的事情 -
Just imagine all the things that happen in the real world --

499
00:27:44,840 --> 00:27:46,660
我们现在的交谈
the conversation we are having now,

500
00:27:46,780 --> 00:27:48,870
我讲话的声音和手势
me producing speech sounds and gestures,

501
00:27:48,980 --> 00:27:51,900
我们大脑在此种表现方式的互动
our brain's interacting in this embodied manner.

502
00:27:52,020 --> 00:27:54,270
那是不可能发生在计算机上的
That could not happen in a computer,

503
00:27:54,380 --> 00:27:55,410
然而 说不定
and yet, it is somehow

504
00:27:55,520 --> 00:27:57,410
生命的灵魂无处不在
the essence of what life is all about.

505
00:27:57,520 --> 00:27:58,850
所以对此我持怀疑态度
So l'm skeptical about that.

506
00:28:00,660 --> 00:28:04,850
大脑要永垂不朽或许实在是过于复杂
The brain might be far too complex to immortalize,

507
00:28:04,960 --> 00:28:09,550
但是没准有一条简单的道路能达到永生 -
but maybe there's a simpler path to eternal life --

508
00:28:09,670 --> 00:28:11,020
并不适合你...
not for you...

509
00:28:11,130 --> 00:28:16,070
而是针对我们从零开始打造的不朽之人
but for immortal beings that we create from scratch.

510
00:28:18,370 --> 00:28:19,490
这个星球上的生命
Life on this planet

511
00:28:19,600 --> 00:28:22,690
已有40亿年的进化史了
has had 4 billion years to evolve.

512
00:28:22,810 --> 00:28:27,130
我们处在物种长链的最后位置上
We are the latest in a long line of species.

513
00:28:27,750 --> 00:28:30,040
我们希望我们长盛不衰
We hope we can last.

514
00:28:30,150 --> 00:28:35,210
但我们对永垂不朽的追求可能会终结于灾难
But our quest for immortality could end in disaster

515
00:28:35,320 --> 00:28:40,510
因为首个永生之人有可能并不是人类
because the first eternal beings may not be human.

516
00:28:40,630 --> 00:28:45,860
他们可能就是要让我们灭绝
And they might just make us extinct.

517
00:28:48,870 --> 00:28:53,030
复杂生命始于一些简单的规律
Complex life began from a few simple laws.

518
00:28:53,140 --> 00:28:57,730
同理完全适合于人造生命
The same might be true for artificial life.

519
00:28:57,840 --> 00:29:00,830
若是人类发现了那些规律 就有机会
lf humans discover those laws, there is a chance

520
00:29:00,950 --> 00:29:04,770
我们能创造出长生不死的活生生的东西
we could create living things that live forever.

521
00:29:04,880 --> 00:29:09,780
牛津大学的物理学家弗拉特科.韦德拉尔 正试图了解
Oxford physicist Vlatko Vedral is trying to understand

522
00:29:09,890 --> 00:29:13,280
智慧是如何出现自一个系统
how intelligence might emerge from a system

523
00:29:13,390 --> 00:29:17,020
其操控只靠一些基本的规则
that operates on just a few basic ground rules.

524
00:29:17,130 --> 00:29:19,150
这是象棋
So, this is chess.

525
00:29:19,260 --> 00:29:20,950
它是个很复杂的游戏
lt's a very complex game,

526
00:29:21,070 --> 00:29:24,990
然而它只受控于几条很简单的规则
and yet it's governed by a few very simple rules.

527
00:29:25,100 --> 00:29:28,630
你看到每一方只有16颗棋子
You really have only 16 pieces on each side,

528
00:29:28,740 --> 00:29:31,100
每颗棋子都可以做二选一
and each piece can do one of two things.

529
00:29:31,210 --> 00:29:33,700
你可以说生命也是同样的道理
And you can say the same thing about life.

530
00:29:33,810 --> 00:29:35,300
尽管你可以说出
Even though you are talking

531
00:29:35,410 --> 00:29:37,870
一两条很简单的规则
about one or two very simple rules,

532
00:29:37,980 --> 00:29:39,070
但是你依然能得到着大量的
you still get this multitude

533
00:29:39,180 --> 00:29:41,880
宇宙中各种千变万化
of different possible behaviors in the Universe.

534
00:29:44,160 --> 00:29:48,050
1970年 有位名叫约翰.康威的数学家
ln 1970, a mathematician named John Conway

535
00:29:48,160 --> 00:29:49,880
采纳了这个想法
ran with this idea

536
00:29:49,990 --> 00:29:52,720
尝试创造人造生命形式
and attempted to create artificial life-forms

537
00:29:52,830 --> 00:29:56,090
任其利用计算机程序
spontaneously using a computer program.

538
00:29:56,200 --> 00:29:58,520
他称其为生命游戏
He called it "The Game of Life."

539
00:29:58,640 --> 00:30:02,360
游戏模拟了人造生命的成长
The game simulates the growth of artificial life,

540
00:30:02,470 --> 00:30:04,230
利用二维网格
using a two-dimensional grid

541
00:30:04,340 --> 00:30:07,430
单个细胞生死未卜
and simple cells that are either dead or alive.

542
00:30:07,550 --> 00:30:12,700
细胞的生死全凭若干基本规则掌控
Whether the cells live or die is governed by a few basic rules.

543
00:30:12,820 --> 00:30:18,310
我们面前有大片细胞
So, we have here in front of us a large cell block,

544
00:30:18,420 --> 00:30:21,980
我们可以看到这一个中央细胞 在这里
and we can see this one central cell, which is on.

545
00:30:22,090 --> 00:30:27,420
我们可以看到只有一个邻居在其左边
And we can see that it has only one neighbor to its left.

546
00:30:27,530 --> 00:30:30,890
细胞关闭 则表示它死了
The cell just switches off, which means that it dies.

547
00:30:31,000 --> 00:30:32,560
它孤独的死了
And it dies of loneliness

548
00:30:32,670 --> 00:30:34,930
因为它的邻居太少了
because it has too few neighbors.

549
00:30:35,040 --> 00:30:38,360
好 现在我们有一个中央细胞 在这里
Well, now we've got a central cell, which is on,

550
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:40,230
不过它有五个邻居
but it has five neighbors.

551
00:30:40,350 --> 00:30:43,240
它死了 因为过度拥挤 人口过剩
lt dies because of overcrowding, overpopulation.

552
00:30:43,350 --> 00:30:44,900
它同样不爽
lt doesn't like it, either.

553
00:30:45,020 --> 00:30:49,840
第三个例子 细胞只有两个邻居 -
ln the third example, a cell with only two neighbors --

554
00:30:49,950 --> 00:30:51,810
细胞就能保持存活
the cell just keeps on living.

555
00:30:51,920 --> 00:30:54,780
它在这种环境中甚至还能走动
lt can even move around in this environment,

556
00:30:54,890 --> 00:30:56,250
能够繁衍
and it can reproduce.

557
00:30:56,360 --> 00:30:59,750
所以这是个很好的例子 定居点完全适合
So this is a good example where the population is just right

558
00:30:59,860 --> 00:31:02,320
细胞保持存活
for the cell to keep on living.

559
00:31:02,430 --> 00:31:05,660
给予足够的时间 越发复杂的模式
Given enough time, increasingly complex patterns

560
00:31:05,770 --> 00:31:08,530
会出现自这套简单的规则
emerge from this simple set of rules.

561
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:12,930
有些甚至会表现出生物体的模样
Some even take on the appearance of living organisms.

562
00:31:13,040 --> 00:31:17,500
约翰.康威曾试图获得关于生命的相同点 -
John Conway tried to make the same point regarding life --

563
00:31:17,620 --> 00:31:19,870
它对我们而言貌似很复杂的
that it looks very complicated to us.

564
00:31:19,980 --> 00:31:22,170
的确 它看上去像是个奇迹
And, indeed, it looks like a miracle

565
00:31:22,290 --> 00:31:23,910
生命在我们周围
that there is life around us,

566
00:31:24,020 --> 00:31:26,180
但事实上 他构建了一个游戏
but, in fact, he constructed a game

567
00:31:26,290 --> 00:31:30,080
它由两三条同时又是很简单的规则构成
which consists of two or three, again, very simple rules,

568
00:31:30,200 --> 00:31:33,320
由此产生了一些很复杂的模式.
and it gives rise to some very complicated patterns.

569
00:31:33,430 --> 00:31:36,320
这些模式需要有多复杂
How complex do these patterns need to be

570
00:31:36,430 --> 00:31:41,360
才能让它们成为有生命的 有智慧的 长生不死的东西呢？
before they become something alive, intelligent, or immortal?

571
00:31:41,470 --> 00:31:45,160
弗拉特科认为人脑是个很好的标准
Vlatko thinks the human brain is a good yardstick

572
00:31:45,280 --> 00:31:48,400
已计算出了它的处理能力
and has calculated its processing power.

573
00:31:48,510 --> 00:31:51,540
大脑目前仍然远强大于
The brain is currently still much more powerful

574
00:31:51,650 --> 00:31:53,170
现有的计算机
than the existing computers.

575
00:31:53,280 --> 00:31:56,480
现有的计算机能做到 你知道的
So the existing computers can do something like, you know,

576
00:31:56,590 --> 00:31:58,840
每秒10的12次方比特
10 to the power of 12 beats per second,

577
00:31:58,960 --> 00:32:01,280
然而我们的大脑仍然在大概能做到
whereas our brain is still probably something

578
00:32:01,390 --> 00:32:03,680
比它快百万倍
like a million times faster than that.

579
00:32:03,800 --> 00:32:05,820
因此你需要有100万台笔记本
So you need 1 million laptops

580
00:32:05,930 --> 00:32:09,050
才能模拟一个人脑
to simulate just a single human brain.

581
00:32:09,170 --> 00:32:12,390
当今的计算机无论如何都没有强大到
Today's computers are nowhere near powerful enough

582
00:32:12,500 --> 00:32:14,090
足以容纳人工智能生命
to house artificial intelligent life.

583
00:32:14,210 --> 00:32:16,700
弗拉特科认为答案有可能用于
Vlatko believes the answer might be to use

584
00:32:16,810 --> 00:32:18,860
全然不同的计算机 -
a completely different type of computer --

585
00:32:18,980 --> 00:32:21,400
量子计算机
the quantum computer.

586
00:32:21,510 --> 00:32:22,970
量子计算机
Quantum computer

587
00:32:23,080 --> 00:32:26,670
本质上是未来的计算技术
is basically the future technology of computation.

588
00:32:26,780 --> 00:32:30,210
该计算机速度很快 原则上
lt's a computer that's so fast, in principle,

589
00:32:30,320 --> 00:32:33,710
目前没有计算机能与其匹敌
that no current computer can compete with it.

590
00:32:33,830 --> 00:32:36,180
屏弃了晶体管
ln place of transistors,

591
00:32:36,290 --> 00:32:39,520
量子设备的运算是用单个原子
quantum devices compute with individual atoms.

592
00:32:39,630 --> 00:32:42,920
摒弃了用一大堆的1和0
And instead of sorting piles of ones and zeroes

593
00:32:43,030 --> 00:32:44,820
来给出对与否的答案
to give yes-and-no answers,

594
00:32:44,940 --> 00:32:46,890
量子计算机里的原子
the atoms in quantum computers

595
00:32:47,010 --> 00:32:50,230
可以是1,0，以及两者之间的一切
can be ones, zeroes, and everything in between,

596
00:32:50,340 --> 00:32:54,430
是以计算可能性出现的
existing as a computational maybe.

597
00:32:54,550 --> 00:32:56,900
量子计算机实际上利用了
The quantum computer really utilizes

598
00:32:57,020 --> 00:33:00,010
叫做叠加的量子效应
the quantum effect known as the super position,

599
00:33:00,120 --> 00:33:01,240
那意味着
which means being

600
00:33:01,350 --> 00:33:04,040
在同一时刻出现许多不同的状态
in many different states at the same time.

601
00:33:04,160 --> 00:33:08,910
这种处理多种状态的能力
This ability to handle a multiplicity of states

602
00:33:09,030 --> 00:33:10,920
让量子计算机
allows quantum computers

603
00:33:11,030 --> 00:33:13,620
同时应对许多重迭问题
to juggle many overlapping problems at once,

604
00:33:13,730 --> 00:33:16,560
就是我们大脑的运行方式
just the way our brains do.

605
00:33:17,900 --> 00:33:21,290
打个比喻 我来为你弹琴...
The analogy would be if l played a chord for you...

606
00:33:24,110 --> 00:33:25,500
...你会同时有
...then you would get

607
00:33:25,610 --> 00:33:29,100
许多种不同的弹奏模式
many different modes played at the same time,

608
00:33:29,210 --> 00:33:30,670
那其实是对应了
which would really correspond

609
00:33:30,780 --> 00:33:33,710
同时出现的许多种状态
to many states being out there simultaneously

610
00:33:33,820 --> 00:33:35,070
全都出现在一个地方
all in one location

611
00:33:35,190 --> 00:33:37,210
量子计算的全部能量
and the full power of quantum computation,

612
00:33:37,320 --> 00:33:39,380
实际上无极限的 某种意义上
which is actually unlimited, in some sense,

613
00:33:39,490 --> 00:33:40,580
就看你是否要储存
if you start to store

614
00:33:40,690 --> 00:33:42,810
0和1之间所有的数值了
all the values in between the zero and the one.

615
00:33:42,930 --> 00:33:47,990
于是你就真正上了一个台阶 你可以编码好多好多
So then you really reach a stage where you can encode much more

616
00:33:48,100 --> 00:33:51,560
可以说是真正做到了无极限
and the capacity really has no limits in that sense.

617
00:33:51,670 --> 00:33:53,020
或许有朝一日
There might be a time

618
00:33:53,140 --> 00:33:56,430
人类放弃了自己的长生之梦
when humans forego their own dream of immortality

619
00:33:56,540 --> 00:34:00,200
创造出永恒的人工生命
and create eternal artificial life.

620
00:34:00,310 --> 00:34:02,070
如是那样
And if they do,

621
00:34:02,180 --> 00:34:05,100
人工生命与生物生命将会如何相处呢？
how would artificial and biological life get along?

622
00:34:05,220 --> 00:34:08,240
有些人认为很快
Some people think that very soon

623
00:34:08,350 --> 00:34:11,740
计算机将会聪明过我们 它们会将我们放入动物园
computers will be smarter than us and they'll put us in zoos.

624
00:34:11,860 --> 00:34:15,410
它们把我们关进笼子 扔花生给我们吃
They'll put us behind bars and throw peanuts at us

625
00:34:15,530 --> 00:34:17,550
让我们在笼子里跳舞
and make us dance behind bars

626
00:34:17,660 --> 00:34:21,290
就像我们今天在动物园里让熊跳舞那样
just like we make bears dance in zoos today.

627
00:34:21,400 --> 00:34:23,490
不过在目前
But at the present time,

628
00:34:23,600 --> 00:34:26,790
这些技术都还是八字没一撇
none of these technologies are ready for prime time.

629
00:34:26,900 --> 00:34:30,330
我们根本不具有可运作的量子计算机
We simply don't have an operating quantum computer.

630
00:34:30,440 --> 00:34:35,170
量子计算机计算的世界纪录是 -
The world's record for a quantum computer calculation is --

631
00:34:35,280 --> 00:34:36,300
ta-da --
ta-da --

632
00:34:36,410 --> 00:34:39,840
3乘以5等于15
3 times 5 is 15.

633
00:34:39,950 --> 00:34:42,770
我们有时会忽视计算机和机器人
We sometimes forget that computers and robots,

634
00:34:42,890 --> 00:34:46,910
无论它们有多先进 作为计算机
no matter how advanced they are, are adding machines.

635
00:34:47,020 --> 00:34:48,110
但那并不意味着
But that doesn't mean

636
00:34:48,230 --> 00:34:51,520
它们具有创造性 想象力 主动性
they have creativity, imagination, initiative.

637
00:34:51,630 --> 00:34:53,890
那并不意味着它们理解了人类的价值观
lt doesn't mean they understand human values.

638
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:57,020
那并不意味着它们可以如我们这样 做出跳跃式的逻辑推理
lt doesn't mean that they can make leaps of logic like we can.

639
00:34:57,140 --> 00:34:59,660
换句话说 我们有很长的路要走
ln other words, we have a long ways to go

640
00:34:59,770 --> 00:35:01,960
我们方始能够模拟
before we can begin to appro ximate

641
00:35:02,070 --> 00:35:05,600
发生在人类身上的真正的思维过程.
the real thinking process that takes place in a human being.

642
00:35:05,710 --> 00:35:09,970
我们躲过了成为量子智能的奴隶...
We're safe from becoming slaves to quantum intelligence...

643
00:35:10,080 --> 00:35:11,500
暂且
for the time being.

644
00:35:11,620 --> 00:35:15,740
但这位物理学家设想了人类的一个不同的命运
But this physicist envisions a different fate for mankind,

645
00:35:15,850 --> 00:35:20,180
介于人工生命与生物生命之间
one where the lines between artificial and biological life

646
00:35:20,290 --> 00:35:21,450
模糊不清
will blur

647
00:35:21,560 --> 00:35:25,020
不朽将成为现实
and immortality will become reality,

648
00:35:25,130 --> 00:35:26,890
不仅对生者...
not just for the living...

649
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:30,430
并且还对死者
but also for the dead.

650
00:35:33,590 --> 00:35:35,440
我们都被灌输过科学思想与宗教
We're taught to think of science and religion

651
00:35:35,560 --> 00:35:37,110
是各不相干的真理
as separate truths.

652
00:35:38,100 --> 00:35:41,220
爱因斯坦并不这么认为
Albert Einstein didn't believe that.

653
00:35:41,340 --> 00:35:45,540
他说过 没有宗教的科学是瘸子
He said, "Science without religion is lame.

654
00:35:45,660 --> 00:35:48,810
没有科学的宗教是瞎子
Religion without science is blind."

655
00:35:48,930 --> 00:35:52,120
实现不朽的秘密
The secret to achieving immortality

656
00:35:52,230 --> 00:35:56,090
可能需要将人类与上帝融合成
could require the fusion of humanity and God

657
00:35:56,200 --> 00:35:59,660
一台永恒的宇宙计算机
into an everlasting cosmic computer.

658
00:35:59,770 --> 00:36:03,660
很少有人思索未来有比弗兰克.提普勒更进一步的
Few people think further into the future than Frank Tipler,

659
00:36:03,770 --> 00:36:05,260
他是一个数学物理学家
a mathematical physicist

660
00:36:05,380 --> 00:36:07,780
任职于新奥尔良杜兰大学
at Tulane University in New Orleans.

661
00:36:07,890 --> 00:36:11,250
弗兰克预测 在我们进化的某些点上
Frank predicts that, at some point in our evolution,

662
00:36:11,360 --> 00:36:13,950
真正不同寻常的情况将会发生
something truly remarkable will happen.

663
00:36:14,060 --> 00:36:20,220
人类 宇宙 上帝会统一起来 -
Humanity, the Universe, and God will unite --

664
00:36:20,340 --> 00:36:24,630
该时刻他称为欧米茄点
a moment he calls the omega point.

665
00:36:24,740 --> 00:36:29,400
欧米茄点就是宇宙的尽头
The omega point is the very end of the Universe.

666
00:36:29,510 --> 00:36:33,470
在这过程中 人类 更确切说 我们的子孙
ln the process, mankind, or, more precisely, our descendants,

667
00:36:33,580 --> 00:36:35,770
将会从这个星球扩散出去
will expand out from this planet

668
00:36:35,890 --> 00:36:40,480
最后吞没了整个宇宙
and ultimately engulf the entire Universe.

669
00:36:40,590 --> 00:36:44,750
随着我们的后裔逐渐进入这个最后的阶段
As our descendants are moving into this final state,

670
00:36:44,860 --> 00:36:49,020
他们的知识 他们的力量 他们的计算能力
their knowledge and their power and their computer capacity

671
00:36:49,130 --> 00:36:52,690
与日俱增 强大无比
is increasing without limit.

672
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:54,090
物理定律
The laws of physics

673
00:36:54,200 --> 00:36:57,130
允许一个过程 就是将物质 -
allow a process that will convert matter --

674
00:36:57,240 --> 00:37:03,000
比如这个墓地里的石头 - 转换成纯能量
stones of this graveyard, for instance -- into pure energy.

675
00:37:03,110 --> 00:37:05,770
那将是终极能源
That will be the ultimate energy source,

676
00:37:05,880 --> 00:37:09,340
我们的后裔将会利用并支配它
which our descendants will use and gain control of it.

677
00:37:09,450 --> 00:37:12,440
到了欧米伽点 弗兰克认为
At the omega point, Frank argues,

678
00:37:12,550 --> 00:37:16,580
我们的后裔将无所不能了
our descendants will be capable of doing anything.

679
00:37:16,690 --> 00:37:21,220
有了无穷的力量 他们能够创造一台宇宙计算机
And with infinite power, they will create a cosmic computer

680
00:37:21,330 --> 00:37:23,120
在模拟中重构
and reconstruct in a simulation

681
00:37:23,230 --> 00:37:25,350
宇宙历史上
everything that has ever happened

682
00:37:25,470 --> 00:37:27,590
所发生过的一切
in the history of the Universe.

683
00:37:27,700 --> 00:37:31,430
现在 站在遥远未来的人类的角度
Now, from the point of view of the beings in the far future,

684
00:37:31,540 --> 00:37:35,460
我们将成为他们的候补祖先 是理性人
we will be their alternate ancestor as rational beings

685
00:37:35,580 --> 00:37:39,500
因此他们将会感兴趣于我们是什么 我们像什么
so they will be interested in what we were, what we were like.

686
00:37:39,620 --> 00:37:42,970
因而 所有的男女老少
As a consequence, every man, woman, and child

687
00:37:43,090 --> 00:37:45,170
都将会被带回到现实生活中
will be brought back into existence.

688
00:37:45,290 --> 00:37:48,380
那就会像是你凭借自己的躯体
lt will be just like you are brought back with your body

689
00:37:48,490 --> 00:37:52,350
被带回到了如我们现在所定居的重建的地球
into a reconstructed Earth just like we now live on.

690
00:37:52,460 --> 00:37:55,790
那将会在一个关键的方面有所不同
lt will be different in one crucial respect.

691
00:37:55,900 --> 00:37:57,220
我们将会复活
We will be resurrected,

692
00:37:57,330 --> 00:38:00,220
但我们将永远不必再次经历死亡
but we will never have to go through death again.

693
00:38:00,340 --> 00:38:02,830
弗兰克声称物理规则
Frank claims the laws of physics

694
00:38:02,940 --> 00:38:06,130
不仅允许此类不朽 -
not only permit this type of immortality --

695
00:38:06,240 --> 00:38:08,900
它们实际上需要此情发生
they actually require it to happen.

696
00:38:09,010 --> 00:38:11,500
热力学第二定律称
Second Law of Thermodynamics says

697
00:38:11,610 --> 00:38:16,240
宇宙在最基本层面上的复杂性
the complexity of the Universe at the most fundamental level

698
00:38:16,350 --> 00:38:18,510
越发没有限制
is increasing without limit.

699
00:38:18,620 --> 00:38:20,410
我的结论
l conclude

700
00:38:20,520 --> 00:38:23,350
热力学第二定律的效力
that the validity of the Second Law of Thermodynamics,

701
00:38:23,460 --> 00:38:25,950
贯穿始终的
throughout all of time,

702
00:38:26,060 --> 00:38:28,350
实际上需要生命
actually requires life

703
00:38:28,460 --> 00:38:32,790
出现以获得对宇宙的控制
to come into existence to gain control of the Universe.

704
00:38:32,900 --> 00:38:36,490
无论不朽出现在数十亿年后
Whether immortality comes in billions of years

705
00:38:36,610 --> 00:38:39,000
还是出现在本世纪
or whether it comes this century,

706
00:38:39,110 --> 00:38:43,500
征服死亡将改变我们的文明 -
the conquest of death will transform our civilization --

707
00:38:43,610 --> 00:38:48,170
我们生活的方式 我们工作的方式 我们热爱的方式
the way we live, the way we work, the way we love.

708
00:38:48,280 --> 00:38:51,470
或许问题并不是 - 我们能长生不死么？
Maybe the question is not "Can we live forever?"

709
00:38:51,590 --> 00:38:53,820
而是 - 我们该长生不死么？
But "Should we?"

710
00:38:57,530 --> 00:38:59,020
亚历山大.罗斯
Alexander Rose

711
00:38:59,130 --> 00:39:02,450
是今日永存基金会的执行董事
is the executive director of the Long Now Foundation,

712
00:39:02,560 --> 00:39:05,320
该组织的主要重点
an organization whose main focus

713
00:39:05,430 --> 00:39:09,430
是打造一个不寻常的时钟
is the building of an unusual timepiece.

714
00:39:09,540 --> 00:39:13,900
它叫做今日永存之钟
lt's called the Clock of the Long Now,

715
00:39:14,010 --> 00:39:19,000
它设计成万年计时
and it's designed to tick for 10,000 years.

716
00:39:19,110 --> 00:39:21,970
我们打造了万年之钟
We built the 10,000-year clock

717
00:39:22,080 --> 00:39:25,070
给人们以真正长期的
to give people a different perspective

718
00:39:25,190 --> 00:39:26,980
别样的展望
of the really long term.

719
00:39:27,090 --> 00:39:29,380
如果你相信医学科学或其它东西
lf you believe that medical science or other things

720
00:39:29,490 --> 00:39:31,280
会增加人的寿命的话
are going to increase human longevity,

721
00:39:31,390 --> 00:39:34,320
那么你就所要接触的世界
then the way that you would approach the world

722
00:39:34,430 --> 00:39:36,090
将会是非常非常异样的 -
would be very, very differently --

723
00:39:36,200 --> 00:39:38,960
同样 如果我们设计一座万年之钟
just the same way that if we're designing a 10,000-year clock

724
00:39:39,070 --> 00:39:42,160
来取代一座只能持续10年的时钟
instead of a clock that just has to last for 10 years,

725
00:39:42,270 --> 00:39:45,230
我们所接触到的这个设计问题非常另类
we have approached this design problem very differently.

726
00:39:45,340 --> 00:39:48,320
我们得在接下来的10000年里
And we have to be responsible over those next 10,000 years

727
00:39:48,440 --> 00:39:49,440
对我们的时钟负起责任
for our clock.

728
00:39:49,550 --> 00:39:51,570
过去10000年
10,000 years in the past,

729
00:39:51,680 --> 00:39:54,670
我们还生活在石器时代
we were still living in the stone age.

730
00:39:54,780 --> 00:39:56,770
快进同样的时间
Flash forward the same period,

731
00:39:56,890 --> 00:39:59,410
我们的文明与科技
and our civilization and technology

732
00:39:59,520 --> 00:40:02,210
将会是面目全非的
will be unrecognizable.

733
00:40:02,320 --> 00:40:04,610
我们意识到要打造万年之钟
We realize in designing a 10,000-year clock

734
00:40:04,730 --> 00:40:08,480
最经久耐用的设计可能就是最简单的
that the most durable design is likely the simplest.

735
00:40:08,600 --> 00:40:12,720
今日永存之钟将是全机械的
The Clock of the Long Now will be all mechanical.

736
00:40:12,840 --> 00:40:15,830
时钟的动力将来自重力
Power in the clock will come from the force of gravity.

737
00:40:15,940 --> 00:40:20,900
重力驱动系统转动一根装饰棒
A weight-driven system will turn a threaded bar.

738
00:40:21,010 --> 00:40:22,800
时钟的走时
And the clock will keep time

739
00:40:22,910 --> 00:40:25,600
既有短期的 利用钟摆
in both the short term, with the pendulum,

740
00:40:25,710 --> 00:40:28,340
还有长期的 通过太阳的同步装置
and in the long term, through a solar synchronizer.

741
00:40:28,450 --> 00:40:32,840
此装置难说能维持万年
This device may or may not last for 10 millennia,

742
00:40:32,960 --> 00:40:36,450
但那并不是亚历山大的真正目的
but that's not really Alexander's point.

743
00:40:36,560 --> 00:40:39,850
他想让我们去想象我们
He wants to make us imagine what we will be like

744
00:40:39,960 --> 00:40:43,190
在10000年后会是啥模样
10,000 years from now.

745
00:40:43,300 --> 00:40:45,290
你看到万年之钟
As soon as you see that 10,000-year clock

746
00:40:45,400 --> 00:40:47,820
你来参观它 马上就会说起
and you visit it, the conversations that you have

747
00:40:47,940 --> 00:40:49,730
是否还会有人类存在
around whether or not humans will be there,

748
00:40:49,840 --> 00:40:52,190
人们会嗤之以鼻 -
the shapes of the hands of the people that might wind it --

749
00:40:52,310 --> 00:40:53,860
他们会和我们一模一样么？
"Are they gonna be the same as ours?"

750
00:40:53,980 --> 00:40:55,160
他们会有所不同么？
"Are they gonna be different?"

751
00:40:55,280 --> 00:40:57,970
那种谈话立刻会让你
Those kind of conversations immediately allow you

752
00:40:58,080 --> 00:41:00,840
对那种时间跨度负起责任来
to take responsibility for that kind of time span,

753
00:41:00,950 --> 00:41:03,040
那就是此钟的希望所在
and that's the hope with this clock.

754
00:41:03,150 --> 00:41:08,180
万年后 人类有可能已经永生了
10,000 years from now, human beings may be immortal.

755
00:41:08,290 --> 00:41:09,610
即使不是
Even if they're not,

756
00:41:09,730 --> 00:41:13,280
他们的寿命也必将大大长于我们
their life-spans will surely be vastly longer than ours.

757
00:41:13,400 --> 00:41:16,520
然而那不只是我们躯体会有所变化
But it won't be just our bodies that will change.

758
00:41:16,630 --> 00:41:21,060
那意味着人类也将有所不同
What it means to be human will be different.

759
00:41:21,170 --> 00:41:23,590
你不妨想想人类所有的价值观
lf you think about all the human values,

760
00:41:23,710 --> 00:41:25,090
其中有许多都出自这样的事实
quite a lot of them come

761
00:41:25,210 --> 00:41:28,000
即我们知道生命是有限的
from the fact that we know that life is finite.

762
00:41:28,110 --> 00:41:32,000
你不妨想想 我是如何感受面前的那个人的
lf you think, "How do l feel towards that person?"

763
00:41:32,110 --> 00:41:33,500
事实上你知道
The fact that you know

764
00:41:33,620 --> 00:41:36,510
此人有朝一日将不在人世了
that the person is not gonna be there one day

765
00:41:36,620 --> 00:41:40,080
或许会让你的情感更加强烈
probably makes your emotions far stronger in some sense

766
00:41:40,190 --> 00:41:42,010
某种意义上会超过他们在世
than other ways they would be.

767
00:41:42,120 --> 00:41:45,180
所以这都会发生变化的 或许其中某些东西
So this could all change, and maybe some of these things

768
00:41:45,290 --> 00:41:48,080
会从人类身上消失
would even disappear from the human race

769
00:41:48,200 --> 00:41:50,490
如果我们只是知道我们能够长生不死的话
if we simply knew we could live forever.

770
00:41:50,600 --> 00:41:53,460
婚姻与关系会发生什么
What happens to marriage and relationships

771
00:41:53,570 --> 00:41:56,330
如果我们的寿命延至千岁乃至更长的话？
if our life-spans grow to 1,000 years or more?

772
00:41:56,440 --> 00:41:57,660
眼下
Right now,

773
00:41:57,770 --> 00:42:00,360
我们半数人在我们的短暂人生中就已经离婚了
half of us get divorced just in our short lifetimes.

774
00:42:00,480 --> 00:42:02,360
但若是我们活到1000岁
But if we lived for 1,000 years,

775
00:42:02,480 --> 00:42:05,670
谁知道你会结婚多少回？
who knows how many times you might get married?

776
00:42:05,780 --> 00:42:08,240
我不认为我能够长生不死
l don't think that l would be able to live forever.

777
00:42:08,350 --> 00:42:10,170
我认为我的老婆会首先把我杀了
l think my wife would kill me first.

778
00:42:10,290 --> 00:42:11,570
不管我们喜欢与否
Whether we like it or not,

779
00:42:11,690 --> 00:42:14,510
越来越多的科学家认为我们会有朝一日
more and more scientists believe we will one day live

780
00:42:14,620 --> 00:42:18,780
活在一个没有年岁 没有疾病 没有死亡的世界里
in a world without age, disease, and death...

781
00:42:18,890 --> 00:42:22,920
我们会陶醉在无限生命的乐趣和奇迹中
that we'll revel in the joys and wonders of endless life

782
00:42:23,030 --> 00:42:26,920
我们就必须学会去应对
and that we'll just have to learn to cope

783
00:42:27,040 --> 00:42:32,760
长生不死的后果
with the consequences of living forever.

784
00:42:32,870 --> 00:42:38,500
神话说 众神羡慕我们的必死性
Mythology says that the gods envy our mortality.

785
00:42:38,610 --> 00:42:40,140
我们的必死性
Our mortality

786
00:42:40,250 --> 00:42:44,070
让生命可贵 让东西有味
is what makes life precious and something to be savored.

787
00:42:44,190 --> 00:42:47,180
在时间压力的驱动下达致伟大
Driven by the pressure of time to achieve greatness,

788
00:42:47,290 --> 00:42:51,410
那可能就是我们的必死性所赋予我们人类的
it may be our mortality that gives us our humanity.

789
00:42:51,530 --> 00:42:53,820
但只要我们是凡人
But as long as we are mortal,

790
00:42:53,930 --> 00:42:57,790
我们就永远都不会停止长生不死之梦
we'll never stop dreaming of life everlasting.

791
00:42:57,900 --> 00:43:02,030
那也就造就了我们人类
That, too, is what makes us human.

792
00:43:02,470 --> 00:43:03,680
-- 感谢观赏 --

